{
  "dom_id": "step-q343404",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The depth $D,$ in meters, of a plant\u2019s root system after $t$ months is modeled by $D(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2t+13}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months?\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $t=6$ months is\n{{field-1}} meters per month.",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months is equal to ${D}^{\u2032}(6).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2t+13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2t+13)^{(1/2)-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2t+13)^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{\u2032}(6) & =\\frac{1}{4}(2(6)+13)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months is $\\frac{1}{20}$ meters per month.",
